Repository: <workspace> (Hono v4.13.18, TypeScript).

TASK: Fix a Set-Cookie merge bug in the `Context.res` setter and cover it with tests. Do NOT run git add/stage/commit; leave changes unstaged.

=== 1. Bug ===
In `src/context.ts`, the `set res(_res: Response | undefined)` setter (around lines 414-434) merges headers from the existing `this.#res` into the newly assigned `_res`. For `set-cookie` it currently does:

```ts
if (k === 'set-cookie') {
  const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
  _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
  for (const cookie of cookies) {
    _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
}
```

The `_res.headers.delete('set-cookie')` call wipes out any Set-Cookie headers that the newly assigned Response already had. So when middleware has set a cookie on `c.res` and a handler returns a new Response with its own Set-Cookie header, only the middleware cookie survives.

=== 2. Required fix ===
Both sets of cookies must survive. Change the `set-cookie` branch so it preserves `_res`'s own cookies AND appends the pre-existing `this.#res` cookies. Preserve ordering with pre-existing (context/middleware) cookies first, then the assigned response's cookies — consistent with `#newResponse`. Implement it as:

```ts
if (k === 'set-cookie') {
  const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
  const assignedCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
  _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
  for (const cookie of cookies) {
    _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
  for (const cookie of assignedCookies) {
    _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
}
```

Keep the rest of the setter unchanged. The `const v` from the `entries()` loop remains unused in this branch, exactly as it is today.

=== 3. Tests in `src/context.test.ts` ===
(a) UPDATE the existing test `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to `c.res`'` (around lines 430-445). It currently asserts the buggy behavior (new cookie dropped). Change its final two expectations to assert that the previously set cookies AND the re-assigned response's cookie all survive, in order:

```ts
expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie().length).toBe(cookies.length + newCookies.length)
expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual([...cookies, ...newCookies])
```

Keep the `newCookies` and `newResponse` setup code as-is.

(b) ADD a new regression test immediately after that test, describing the real middleware + handler scenario:

```ts
it('Should keep cookies set by middleware and by a newly returned Response', () => {
  // Middleware has already set a cookie on `c.res`
  c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', 'middleware=1; Path=/')

  // A handler returns a new Response carrying its own Set-Cookie header
  const handlerResponse = new Response('OK', {
    headers: { 'content-type': 'text/plain' },
  })
  handlerResponse.headers.append('set-cookie', 'handler=1; Path=/')

  c.res = handlerResponse

  const cookies = c.res.headers.getSetCookie()
  expect(cookies.length).toBe(2)
  expect(cookies.includes('middleware=1; Path=/')).toBe(true)
  expect(cookies.includes('handler=1; Path=/')).toBe(true)
})
```

Match the file's existing style (global describe/it/expect, no new imports needed).

=== 4. Verification ===
Run exactly:

  cd <workspace> && pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts

If it fails, diagnose and fix only the code/test you changed; do not weaken the assertions. Report:
- The exact diff you produced (files + changed lines).
- Full pass/fail summary of the test command, including the test count.
- Any conflicts or uncertainties.

Do not modify any other files or unrelated tests.

